    "text": "The distinction between algorithmic error and legal deception depends on whether the defect was foreseeable and preventable by a human actor with a duty of care.",
    "topic": "boundary criterion"
  },
  {
    "text": "Algorithmic error can possess criminal character if it results from recklessness or intentional disregard for safety in system design or data curation.",
    "topic": "criminal character scope"
  },
  {
    "text": "Systematic evaluation fraud, where known failure modes are concealed, reclassifies subsequent algorithmic errors as legal deception because they are attributable to intentional human misrepresentation.",
    "topic": "evaluation fraud reclassification"
  },
  {
    "text": "The definition of algorithmic error as purely technical defect presupposes a context of good-faith development, absent from environments with widespread evaluation malpractices.",
    "topic": "contextual precondition"
  }
]
